. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * In the PAP, the results should be presented in a single table with 
> one column. 
.         * Our RT misunderstood and thought it implied a single table with mul
> tiple columns (and thus the possibility of having multiple estimations). 
.         * Accordingly, of the four models proposed in the PAP, we generated a
> nd estimated, we chose to extract results only for the control model (Model 2
> ) 
.         * and not the age-group effects models (Models 3 and 4). 
.         * We present the code for the others models but their results were no
> t extracted.
.         
.         * We consider a: logistic regression
. 
.         * Let's assume that the measure we built previsously based on plh0004
>  is a good measure of left leaning political preferences.
.         * If having a firstborn child female, the respondent is more likely t
> o having left leaning political preferences (1 left, 0 right).
.         * Accordingly, we run a logistic regression with left_pol_preferences
>  as the dependent variable and firstborn_child_female, respondent_female (sex
> ), age_2019
.         *(and its square), and education_level as independent variables, usin
> g analytic weights (phrf) and clustering standard errors by hid.
. 
.         **In the PAP: model 2
.         logit left_pol_preferences firstborn_child_female respondent_female a
> ge_2019 age_square_2019 ib0.education_level [pweight=phrf], cluster(hid)

note: 0.education_level != 0 predicts failure perfectly;
      0.education_level omitted and 1 obs not used.

note: 3.education_level omitted because of collinearity.
